      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The grant</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court agreed to decide whether the government must hold, while their removal cases are pending, people who are living in the United States and were never admitted. The order takes the case; it does not decide it. The detention statute deems a person present in the United States who has not been admitted an applicant for admission. The bond statute says the government "may release" a person on bond.<Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="THURSDAY, OCTOBER 1, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="RHONEY, ACTING DIR. V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA, RICARDO A." /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /><Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." /><Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="An alien present in the United States who has not been admitted" /><Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="shall be deemed for purposes of this chapter an applicant for admission." /><Cite s="uscode-2024-1226" passage="may release the alien on" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">For about thirty years, by the Second Circuit's account, people who entered without inspection and were not admitted or paroled, and who were not apprehended while entering or shortly afterward or held on a criminal or terrorism ground, could ask for bond. On July 10, 2025, a memorandum from the commissioner of Customs and Border Protection said the detention statute covers them instead. On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals said immigration judges lack authority to grant them bond. On September 26, 2025, officers arrested Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts. An immigration judge denied him a bond hearing. A district court ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days. An immigration judge released him on bond after finding no danger and no flight risk. On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit ruled the other way from the Board in Barbosa da Cunha's case.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Executive Branch practice over thirty years" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="without inspection and admission" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="detain noncitizens on certain criminal or terrorism-related grounds" /><Cite s="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" passage="aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="in July 2025, the government changed that long-settled practice" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" passage="on July 10, 2025" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="FROM: Rodney S. Scott" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236, is the applicable immigration detention authority for all applicants for admission." /><Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" passage="Immigration Judges lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Petitioner-Appellee Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="arrested Petitioner while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Petitioner requested a bond hearing, which an immigration judge denied based on a determination that he was subject to mandatory detention" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="The district court granted the petition and ordered the government to either provide a bond hearing or release Petitioner within ten days." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="presents no danger to persons or property or risk of flight, released him on bond." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The detention statute uses two descriptions of the person, an applicant for admission and an alien seeking admission. It tells an immigration officer to detain that person for a removal proceeding when the officer decides the person seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.<Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Detention statute" when="2024 compilation" where="Inspection of applicants for admission" label="Government Publishing Office">
          <p>in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained<Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Before 1996, the procedures for detaining and expelling a person depended largely on whether the person had physically entered the United States. Someone who entered illegally without being caught was eligible for bond. Someone who presented for inspection at a port of entry was to be detained unless clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to enter, or unless granted parole. In 1996 Congress enacted the removal and detention provisions this case concerns. The government's petition says Congress acted to correct that disparity. Congress replaced the defined term entry with admission: the lawful entry of a person into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer. Members of Congress described lawful admission, rather than physical entry, as the pivotal factor. The government contends that Barbosa da Cunha is subject to mandatory detention because he is an applicant for admission who is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted. It argues that "the ordinary meanings of the phrases 'applicant for admission' and 'seeking admission' are the same."<Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Before 1996, the applicable procedures for detaining and expelling an alien under the INA depended largely on whether the alien had physically “entered” the United States" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Under that physical-entry-based regime, aliens who managed to evade detection and physically “enter” the United States illegally were subject to deportation proceedings and eligible for bond." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Conversely, aliens who presented themselves for inspection at a port of entry on American soil were deemed not to have “entered” the United States." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Those aliens therefore were subject to exclusion proceedings, and to mandatory detention during such proceedings." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="except those “clearly and beyond a doubt entitled” to enter" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="“shall be detained” for further inquiry, unless they were granted parole." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="This case concerns several INA provisions addressing removal and detention that were enacted in 1996 as part of the Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="In IIRIRA, Congress acted to correct that disparity." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Congress replaced the defined term “entry” with the term" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="“admission” is defined by statute as “the lawful entry of the alien into the United States after inspection and authorization.”" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="entry of the alien into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Instead of making physical entry into the United States the touchstone of an alien’s status, the “pivotal factor” became “whether or not the alien has been lawfully admitted.”" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="The government contends that Petitioner is subject to mandatory detention because he is an “applicant for admission” who “is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.”" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="the ordinary meanings of the phrases ‘applicant for admission’ and ‘seeking admission’ are the same." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Barbosa da Cunha's response, dated August 21, says that in the detention provisions Congress largely kept the earlier scheme. On that account, people arrested inside the country, including "those who had entered without admission," remained eligible for release on bond, and people apprehended at the border continued to be detained without the possibility of bond. He says Congress kept that difference even as the law changed the removal process, combining deportation and exclusion into one removal procedure and making the grounds depend on admission rather than on physical entry. He says the law also deemed certain people applicants for admission, including people present in the United States who had not been admitted, which put people who entered without inspection on the same footing in removal proceedings as other applicants for admission. Even with those changes, he says, Congress kept the core of the earlier detention scheme, including release on bond for people who had entered without inspection and detention without bond for people seeking admission at the border.<Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="Dated: August 21, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="In IIRIRA’s detention statutes, Congress largely preserved the preexisting detention scheme" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="those who had entered without admission, remained eligible for release on bond" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="Those apprehended at the border continued to be detained without possibility of bond" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="even as IIRIRA made changes to the removal process." /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="combining them into a single “removal” procedure" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="not on physical entry into the United States, but instead on “admission,”" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="“deemed” certain categories of noncitizens “applicant[s] for admission,”" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="present in the United States [without having] been admitted" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="This put noncitizens who entered without inspection on the same footing in removal proceedings as other “applicants for admission,”" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="it retained the core of the pre-IIRIRA detention scheme." /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="previously authorized “release on bond” for individuals who entered without inspection." /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="Congress retained the prior rule of detaining, without possibility of bond, individuals who are “seeking admission” at the border" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The Second Circuit held that Barbosa da Cunha may be an applicant for admission but is not seeking admission, because he is not requesting lawful entry after inspection and authorization; he was already living here. So the court held that the detention statute does not apply to someone in the United States after entering without inspection and admission who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter. The Second Circuit affirmed the district court, which had required a bond hearing or release. Under the July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Parole is a separate power. The parole statute lets the government release a person "only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit". The July 10 memorandum says the only people eligible for release on bond are people already admitted.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage={`Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”`} /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="because he is not requesting lawful entry into the United States after inspection and authorization" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to noncitizens, such as Petitioner, who are present in the United States after entering the country without inspection and admission" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus." /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="The only aliens eligible for a custody determination and release on recognizance, bond, or conditional parole under INA § 236(a) are aliens admitted to the United States" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit" when="April 28, 2026" where="Opinion" label="Barbosa da Cunha">
          <p>Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage={`Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”`} /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Barbosa da Cunha entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. He has never been arrested for or charged with a crime. If the government wins, people in his situation cannot be released on bond under the detention statute. The Second Circuit wrote that if the detention statute applies, he "must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond". The question the Court agreed to decide is whether the detention statute requires that detention. Whether the Constitution separately requires a hearing is not part of the question. The Fourth Circuit names eight courts, the First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh, that held the bond statute applies to noncitizens inside the country, like the people who filed the cases, and that they must be given a bond hearing. On September 10, 2026, that court joined them. The Fifth and the Eighth held that the detention statute applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted, and that they must be detained without bond.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="lived in the United States for more than twenty years after entering the country" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="applied for asylum in 2016 and was granted a valid work permit." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="has never been arrested for or charged with a crime." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond" /><Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="In addition to the statutory question presented in this case, Lopez-Campos also squarely presents the question whether applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s mandatory-detention scheme to such aliens comports with due process." /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="Decided: September 10, 2026" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="The First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits concluded that Section 1226 applies to noncitizens inside our country" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="like the Petitioners, and that they must thus be provided a bond hearing." /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="Today we join the overwhelming majority of circuit courts of appeal that have considered this question" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="The Fifth and Eighth Circuits ruled, on the other hand, that Section 1225 applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted to the United States" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="they must be detained without bond" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The government's petition asked the Court to wait on this case. It said the Court should hold this petition pending another petition, Lopez-Campos, which it called a "better vehicle" for resolving whether holding those people is lawful, and then dispose of this petition as appropriate. The October 1 order granted this petition.<Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="The Court should hold the petition in this case pending the resolution of the petition in Lopez-Campos and then dispose of this petition as appropriate." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Lopez-Campos is a better vehicle than this case for resolving the lawfulness of applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A) to aliens who are present in the country without having been admitted." /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The Court's order list for Thursday, October 1, 2026, grants the petitions for review in a group that includes Philip L. Rhoney, acting director, against Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha. The petition names Rhoney as Acting Field Office Director of the Buffalo Field Office of Immigration and Customs Enforcement, substituted for his predecessor.<Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="THURSDAY, OCTOBER 1, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="RHONEY, ACTING DIR. V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA, RICARDO A." /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Philip L. Rhoney, Acting Field Office Director of the Buffalo Field Office of U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Acting Field Office Director Rhoney is automatically substituted for his predecessor." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The question the Court agreed to hear asks whether the detention statute requires holding, during removal proceedings, people who are in the United States and were not admitted.<Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="present in the United States without having been admitted" /><Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="CERT. GRANTED 10/1/2026" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The Court's list of cases set for argument is dated October 1, 2026. This case is on it with a grant date of October 1. That line does not set a day for argument.<Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="As of October 1, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="RHONEY V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA" /><Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="Granted: 10/1/26" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The docket, opened July 23, 2026, records the petition that day, a response from Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha filed August 21, a reply from Philip L. Rhoney filed September 4, distribution for the September 28 conference, and a grant on October 1. On July 23 the same page said filings in this case should be submitted in paper form only, and should not be submitted through the Court's electronic filing system. The text of that response was posted on October 5. The docket lists that filing under August 21 and notes the PDF upload on October 5, and that entry includes a main document. That document is his response to the petition, dated August 21. The reply of September 4 is still listed, and the docket does not include its text. After the grant, the page says that if the Court schedules briefing and oral argument, the parties shall submit electronic versions of all prior and subsequent filings, and that subsequent filings should now be submitted through the electronic filing system.<Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="Docketed: July 23, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="Aug 21 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="Brief of respondent Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha filed." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="Sep 04 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="Reply of petitioner Philip L. Rhoney filed." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="DISTRIBUTED for Conference of 9/28/2026." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="Petition GRANTED." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="should be submitted in paper form only, and should not be submitted through the Court's electronic filing system." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="If the Court schedules briefing and oral argument" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="the parties shall submit electronic versions of all prior and subsequent filings" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="Subsequent party and amicus filings in the case should now be submitted through the Court’s electronic filing system" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="Brief of respondent Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha filed. (PDF upload - October 5, 2026)" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05" passage="https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/26/26-104/428500/20261005150833296_395847_tst_BRIEF.pdf" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="RESPONSE TO PETITION FOR A WRIT OF CERTIORARI" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="Dated: August 21, 2026" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Officers arrested him on September 26, 2025, while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts. A judge then ordered a bond hearing or release, and an immigration judge released him on bond.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="On September 26, 2025, officers from U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Norwood, Massachusetts" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="provide him with a bond hearing or release him" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>CBS's tens of thousands</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants during removal proceedings without bond hearings. The October 1 order, the Supreme Court's docket for this case, the Second Circuit's opinion, the Board of Immigration Appeals decision, and the July 10 Customs memorandum do not give a precise count of people held under the government's reading. The Fourth Circuit says district courts sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases, which counts court cases, not people in detention.<Cite s="cbs-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="detain tens of thousands of immigrants during their removal proceedings without access to bond hearings" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Who the policy reaches</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Customs and Border Protection's public notice describes mandatory detention for applicants for admission the Department of Homeland Security encounters for the first time after they fail to lawfully present for inspection at a port of entry. The July 10 memorandum says the first encounter counts "regardless of when or where that first encounter occurs". So the policy reaches a person arrested inside the country years after entering: Barbosa da Cunha was arrested driving to work in Norwood more than twenty years after he entered.<Cite s="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" passage="for the first time after failing to lawfully present for inspection at a U.S. port of entry" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="regardless of when or where that first encounter occurs." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="arrested Petitioner while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="lived in the United States for more than twenty years after entering the country" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The practice</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The 1997 Federal Register rule made people present without having been admitted or paroled eligible for bond.</strong></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Date", "Record"]} rows={chronology.rows} ariaLabel="Dates from the 1997 bond rule to the grant of review" />
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">On March 6, 1997, a Federal Register rule, effective April 1, said people present without having been admitted or paroled would be eligible for bond. On September 25, 2026, the full Second Circuit refused to rehear the case. Judge Steven J. Menashi dissented, arguing that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing. Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted against rehearing while writing that he agreed with the dissent's analysis. He wrote that mere substantive disagreement with the panel opinion does not warrant the time and effort of full court rehearing.<Cite s="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" passage="Thursday, March 6, 1997" /><Cite s="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" passage="Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination." /><Cite s="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" passage="Effective date: This interim rule is effective April 1, 1997." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" passage="09/25/2026" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" passage="rehearing in banc is hereby DENIED." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" passage="dissents by opinion from the denial of rehearing in banc." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="The government and Judge Menashi in his dissent from the denial of rehearing en banc argue that Section 1225 applies and that Petitioner must be detained without a bond hearing." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="Although I agree with the analysis in the dissent, I support the decision to deny rehearing en banc." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="My “[m]ere substantive disagreement” with the panel opinion does not warrant the time and effort of full court rehearing." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.<Cite s="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" passage="Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit affirmed the district court. It held that detention of a person in the United States after entering without inspection and admission, who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter, follows the bond statute, not mandatory detention. Judge Cabranes agreed and filed a separate opinion. The appeal was argued April 6.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Decided: April 28, 2026" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Argued: April 6, 2026" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Judge Cabranes concurs in the judgment and opinion of the Court and files a separate opinion." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" kind="record" detail>
          <p>In July 2025 the government changed its practice. The opinion cites immigration enforcement guidance dated July 8, 2025. The petition names that guidance as Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of Immigration and Customs Enforcement, and says applicants for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Divided panels in the Fifth and Eighth Circuits agreed with the government.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025)" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025)." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="may not be released from ICE custody” except by “parole”" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Buenrostro-Mendez v. Bondi, 166 F.4th 494, 508 (5th Cir. 2026)" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Avila v. Bondi, 170 F.4th 1128, 1138 (8th Cir. 2026)" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 25, 2026, the Second Circuit denied rehearing by the full court. Judges Bianco and Nathan, joined by four others, concurred in that denial. Judge Menashi dissented.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" passage="rehearing in banc is hereby DENIED." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" passage="dissents by opinion from the denial of rehearing in banc." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals held that immigration judges lack authority to hear bond requests, or to grant bond, for people present in the United States without admission.<Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" passage="Decided September 5, 2025" /><Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" passage="lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The signed memorandum, a one-page scan, is addressed from Commissioner Rodney S. Scott. It says the department's position is that applicants for admission are subject to mandatory detention and may not be released except by parole.<Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="FROM: Rodney S. Scott" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="SUBJECT: Detention of Applicants for Admission" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="subject to mandatory detention under INA" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6">No argument date has been set. The Court took one question, what the detention statute means. Whether holding someone without a bond hearing violates the Constitution's guarantee of due process is not part of it; the government's petition says a second case, Lopez-Campos from the Sixth Circuit, raises that question as well. The Court had not granted or denied review in that case or in a third, Buenrostro-Mendez from the Fifth Circuit, as of October 3.<Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="RHONEY V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA" /><Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="Granted: 10/1/26" /><Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="In addition to the statutory question presented in this case, Lopez-Campos also squarely presents the question whether applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s mandatory-detention scheme to such aliens comports with due process." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-03" passage="United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03" passage="United States Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Customs and Border Protection's public notice says Commissioner Rodney S. Scott issued guidance on July 10, 2025, and that applicants for admission encountering the department for the first time after failing to lawfully present for inspection at a port of entry are subject to mandatory detention.<Cite s="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" passage="on July 10, 2025" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" passage="subject to mandatory detention under INA Section 235" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof">
        <summary>The question, the statutes, and one count in the coverage</summary>
        <p>The question presented reads: whether the cited detention provision mandates detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like the respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted.<Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." /></p>
        <p>The 2024 compilation of the detention section says that if the examining officer determines an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained for a removal proceeding. The bond section says the government may release an arrested alien on bond of at least $1,500.<Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained" /><Cite s="uscode-2024-1226" passage="bond of at least $1,500" /></p>
        <p>{computed.daysPetitionToGrant} days from the July 23 petition to the October 1 grant. {computed.daysGuidanceToGrant} days from the July 8 guidance date in the opinion to the grant. {computed.daysCbpMemoToGrant} days from the July 10 Customs guidance to the grant. {computed.daysBoardToGrant} days from the September 5 Board decision to the grant. Counted from those dates.</p>
        <p>CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants without bond hearings. That count is not in the October 1 order, the Supreme Court's docket for this case, the Second Circuit's opinion, the Board of Immigration Appeals decision, or the July 10 Customs memorandum.<Cite s="cbs-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="detain tens of thousands of immigrants during their removal proceedings without access to bond hearings" /></p>
        <p>The Associated Press wrote that the Court agreed on Thursday to review a policy denying bond hearings. The Hill wrote that the Supreme Court announced Thursday it will decide the legality of President Trump's mandatory detention policy.<Cite s="ap-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="agreed to review a Trump administration policy denying bond hearings" /><Cite s="hill-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="announced Thursday it will decide the legality" /></p>
      </details>
